BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Roots of the Valleys: A Photography Exhibit
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260729T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260729T170000
UID:260708K-L9Noq4m0yAkQoHrY8skw
DESCRIPTION:**The San Geronimo Valley Community Center proudly presents: R
 oots of the Valleys Photography Exhibition in the Maurice Del Mue Gallerie
 s from July 7 - 31.  There will be a reception for the artists on July 12
 \, from 4 - 7 pm.** \n\n **Roots of the Valleys** brings together the wor
 k of 20 local professional and amateur photographers in a collective explo
 ration of the San Geronimo and Nicasio Valleys. Through images of landscap
 e\, people\, wildlife\, architecture\, and everyday life\, the exhibition 
 reflects the distinctive character\, history\, and sense of place that def
 ine these rural communities. Together\, these diverse perspectives create 
 a visual portrait of the valleys and the connections that root us to them.
 \n\n Artists Include:\n\nGisela Alvarado\, John Andrew Murphy\, Velvy App
 leton\, Marian Cremin\, Cindy Davis\, Donn DeAngelo\, Jerry Draper\, Julie
  Egger\, Martha Elizabeth Ture\, Roberta Floden\, Harlan Floyd\, Sr.\, Dav
 id Glaubinger\, Sasha Gulish\, Amena Hajjar\, Mia Johnson-Martinez\, Sarah
  Killingsworth\, Richard Lang\, David Lee Hoffman\, Avis Licht\, Priscilla
  Patey\, Laura Persson\, Todd Pickering\, Taylor Rechtschaffen\, Juliette 
 Valente
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/roots-of
 -the-valleys-photography-exhibit
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Howl at the Moon Hike at Jack London State Historic Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260729T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260729T230000
UID:260630Qy8tpF7jI0i7ZI6kSs1s9Q
DESCRIPTION:This rare midweek evening hike ventures to the summit of Jack 
 London State Historic Park under the rising Buck Moon.\n\nAt the height of
  summer\, July’s Buck Moon is named for the time when deer are growing n
 ew antlers in velvet\, a sign of seasonal strength. At Jack London State H
 istoric Park\, it also echoes “The Call of the Wild\,” where Buck is t
 he central character—a resilient dog shaped by instinct and endurance. T
 ogether\, these meanings connect the moon above with the surrounding lands
 cape.\n\nHikers will set out in the late afternoon light and reach the sum
 mit as the sun sets to watch the Buck Moon rise. The return hike will be u
 nder moonlight—an entirely different way to experience the trail\, where
  the park feels just a little wilder.\n\nThis is a strenuous hike of 8\\+ 
 miles roundtrip. Participants should be prepared for a steady pace\, limit
 ed stops on the ascent\, and hiking in the dark on the return. A headlamp 
 or flashlight is required for the return hike after dark\, and hiking pole
 s are strongly recommended.\n\nPlease bring plenty of water and a snack or
  bagged meal to enjoy at the summit. Dress in weather-appropriate layers a
 nd wear sturdy hiking shoes. Don’t have a headlamp or trekking poles?  
 Park staff will have a limited number available for checkout on a first-co
 me\, first-served basis.\n\nPet dogs \\(non-service animals\\) are not per
 mitted on backcountry trails in order to protect wildlife. For more inform
 ation about dogs at Jack London State Historic Park\, visit https://jacklo
 ndonpark.com/policies/.
LOCATION:Jack London State Historic Park
URL:https://jacklondonpark.com/events/buck-moon-hike/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Introduction to Acrylic Painting
